The kofun is located on the southeastern slope of a hill on the left bank of the [[:en:Abukuma River|Abukuma River]] in southern Fukushima Prefecture. It lies 400 meters southeast of {{ill|Yachikubo Kofun|ja|谷地久保古墳|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yachikubo Kofun|qq=}} in the same valley. The upper and eastern parts of the mound have been flattened, and an excavation survey was conducted in 2008.

The mound is constructed in two tiers. The first tier is square (16 meters on each side), while the second tier is circular with a diameter of approximately 10 meters, typeing the "Jōen-kahō" type. The slopes of the mound feature a stone-pile construction using river stones, and the flat areas are covered with gravel paving.{{Sfn|Shirakawa City designation1 Pamphlet|2011}} Surrounding the mound is a trench-like outer moat structure. The burial facility is a Cave Tomb-shiki sekaku. While only the floor stone remains in its original position, four other stones are scattered nearby.{{Sfn|Shirakawa City designation1 Pamphlet|2011}} The front yard of the burial chamber opens in a V-type to the south, with walls made of upright river stones and the floor paved with gravel.{{Sfn|Shirakawa City designation1 Pamphlet|2011}} Due to looting, the accompanying grave goods are not well-documented.{{Sfn|Shirakawa City designation1 Pamphlet|2011}}

Nojikubo Kofun is dated to the late [[:en:7th century|7th century]] to early [[:en:8th century|8th century]] and is recognized as one of the few remaining examples of a Jōen-kahō-fun. Along with {{ill|Yachikubo Kofun|ja|谷地久保古墳|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yachikubo Kofun|qq=}}, it features a distinctive Cave Tomb-shiki sekaku, characteristic of the Yamato region. The tomb's occupant is presumed to have been a prominent leader in the ancient [[:en:Nishishirakawa District, Fukushima|Shirakawa District]].{{Sfn|Shirakawa City designation1 Pamphlet|2011}}

The kofun is part of the [[:en:Shirakawa Funada-Motonuma Sites|Shirakawa Funada Motonuma Ruins]] and was Historic site a National Historic site in 2010.

